    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6217A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the surrogate's court procedure act,\nin relation to the appointment of a standby guardian due to\nadministrative separation\n\n PURPOSE OF BILL :\n\nTo allow parents and guardians who become subject to an administrative\nseparation the ability to designate a Standby Guardian.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend section 1726 of the Surrogate's\nCourt Procedure Act to expand the circumstances when a parent or\nguardian may designate a Standby Guardian.\n\nSection 2 sets forth the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCurrently, SCPA § 1726 allows a parent to designate a standby guardian\nfor their child in the event of their (i) incapacity, (ii)\ndebilitation and consent, or (iii) death. This proposed amendment\nseeks to broaden Section 1726 by allowing for a standby guardian's\nauthority to take effect in the event of an \"administrative\nseparation,\" between a parent and their child. Separation is defined\nas a suspension of care between parent and child caused by\nfamily-shattering events including in the event of arrest, detention,\nincarceration, removal and/or deportation, in connection with a\nfederal immigration matter. The legislation also provides the court\nwith the discretion to appoint an attorney for the child to recommend\nwhether the appointment of a standby guardian is in the best interest\nof the child.\n\nProviding parents and guardians with legal tools to plan for the\nsmooth transition of care for their children in the event of a major\nlife disruption is a crucially important step in ensuring stability in\nthe children's lives. SCPA § 1726 was originally enacted by the\nLegislature at the height of the AIDS crisis. The statute was\ndeveloped to facilitate, among other things, the immediate transfer of\ntemporary guardianship powers until a more permanent arrangement and\nhearing can take place.\n\nProviding parents with the ability to make a standby designation that\nonly takes effect upon a triggering event, allows parents to plan, but\nnot relinquish parental rights, and is in the best interests of\nchildren. By any measure, SCPA § 1726 has been an extraordinary\nsuccess in New York and more than half the states have adopted similar\nstatutes.\n\nOver the many years since it became law, it has provided relief for\ncountless parents and helped the designated standby guardians to\nbetter negotiate complex transitions for the children in their care.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
